<!-- Website teaching layer for android/kotlin-multiplatform/ecosystem/dependency-injection.md. Original preserved in full. Structure defined in android-phase9-structure.md before drafting. Checked 2026-10-25 against https://kotlinlang.org/docs/api-guidelines-build-for-multiplatform.html. -->

<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">The shared repository is portable, but constructing it needs an Android database driver in one app and an Apple driver in the other. Dependency injection is the act of supplying those collaborators. Choosing a container is a separate decision.</p>

Start at each application’s composition root and follow construction, ownership and cleanup.

<!-- @after 1. Put the Composition Boundary at the Platform Edge :: visual -->
### Construction belongs at the edge

<figure class="viz"><table><caption>An object graph does not choose its own lifetime</caption><tbody><tr><th scope="row">1</th><td>Platform root: selects implementation and configuration</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">2</th><td>Resource handles: client and driver with explicit owners</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">3</th><td>Shared service: receives constructor dependencies</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">4</th><td>Teardown: dependent work ends before owned resources close</td></tr></tbody></table><figcaption>The boundaries in this example are different responsibilities, not implied packet transfers.</figcaption></figure>

Imagine the shared repository needs an HTTP client and a database driver. The Android and Apple application roots select the platform implementations and pass them through constructors. Shared business logic can then depend on the contracts it actually uses. Tests can substitute a deterministic repository collaborator without constructing platform UI or starting a global container.

For every created resource, record who closes it and which work may still use it. A screen leaving should not close an application-owned client that another feature is using. An application-owned graph should not retain screen callbacks indefinitely either. End dependent work before releasing its owned resources, following those resources’ actual APIs. Constructor type checking verifies compatible inputs; it does not establish cancellation, disposal order or a framework’s scope semantics. Select a DI framework when its concrete benefits justify its target and lifecycle integration cost.

<!-- @after 4. Manual DI Is a First-Class Option :: animation -->
### Build and close one owned graph

<figure class="stepper" data-no-handoff><span class="stepper-title">Build and close one owned graph</span><div class="stage viz-cols" style="--cols:2"><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Contract and use</span><div class="node" data-el="n0"><strong>Platform root</strong><small data-text="v0">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="n1"><strong>Resources</strong><small data-text="v1">—</small></div></div><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Platform and ownership</span><div class="node" data-el="n2"><strong>Shared service</strong><small data-text="v2">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="n3"><strong>Lifetime</strong><small data-text="v3">—</small></div></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[{"label": "Configure", "caption": "The application chooses platform inputs and configuration. Tests can supply different collaborators through the same constructors.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "active", "n1": "idle", "n2": "idle", "n3": "idle"}, "text": {"v0": "Owns setup", "v1": "Not created", "v2": "Not created", "v3": "Explicit owner"}}, {"label": "Construct", "caption": "Create the engine/driver and pass them into the shared graph. The example does not require a global service locator.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "idle", "n1": "active", "n2": "idle", "n3": "idle"}, "text": {"v0": "Supplies dependencies", "v1": "Target-owned handles", "v2": "Receives collaborators", "v3": "Ownership documented"}}, {"label": "Use", "caption": "The shared service uses its declared dependencies. Manual wiring checks constructor types; runtime resource behaviour still needs tests.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "idle", "n1": "idle", "n2": "active", "n3": "idle"}, "text": {"v0": "No hidden lookup", "v1": "Alive while needed", "v2": "Domain operation", "v3": "Active"}}, {"label": "Close", "caption": "At the documented graph lifetime boundary, close resources according to their APIs and ownership order. Do not rely on a UI re-render as teardown.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "idle", "n1": "idle", "n2": "idle", "n3": "active"}, "text": {"v0": "Coordinates release", "v1": "Deterministic cleanup", "v2": "No further use", "v3": "Closed and tested"}}]</script><figcaption>Illustrative sequence, not a measured trace. <a href="https://kotlinlang.org/docs/api-guidelines-build-for-multiplatform.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Primary contract</a>.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after 4. Manual DI Is a First-Class Option :: check -->
### Try the decision yourself

<details><summary>Is Dagger reflective by design?</summary><p>No. Its ordinary graph implementation is generated; its target support is a separate concern.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does a graph compile prove native startup and cleanup?</summary><p>No. Runtime configuration, target composition, resource lifetime and consumer integration need evidence.</p></details>
<details><summary>Must common classes call a container?</summary><p>Constructor injection can keep their dependencies explicit without container access.</p></details>

